/S /P
/P 926 0 R
/E (Over-Dilution of Founders: Giving away too much equity to early advisors, consultants, or non-essential hires too quickly. This can leave founders with insufficient 'dry powder' to incentivise future key hires or satisfy investors in later funding rounds.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
193 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 927 0 R
/E (Ignoring Tax Implications: Failing to consult with tax professionals could expose employee recipients to unexpected and significant tax liabilities as their equity vests.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 928 0 R
/E (Equal Splits Without Merit: Automatically defaulting to equal equity splits among co-founders regardless of their respective contributions, risk exposure, or expertise. This often leads to resentment and performance issues if one partner carries a disproportionate amount of the workload.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 929 0 R
/E (Forgetting about 'Dead Equity': Failing to include buy-back clauses in shareholder agreements. If a founder leaves the company, the business should have the right to repurchase their un-vested \(and sometimes vested\) shares to prevent 'dead equity' from being held by someone no longer contributing.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
